{
  "teamId": 135,
  "year": 2025,
  "narrative": "The 2025 San Diego Padres arrived as a team with something to prove, and for much of the spring and summer they delivered. A 90-win season and a Wild Card berth represented genuine progress, built on a pitching staff that posted a 3.63 ERA and a lineup that found timely ways to manufacture runs even when the power numbers — 152 home runs, a modest total — didn't overwhelm anyone. The identity of this club was grit, depth, and the belief that Petco Park's run-suppression environment could make them competitive in almost any game they played.\n\nThe first months of the season told a story of a team still finding itself. Xander Bogaerts delivered clutch hits that swung early games, Gavin Sheets emerged as an unexpected power presence, and Manny Machado continued to anchor the lineup with veteran poise. But the bullpen was a persistent source of anxiety. Jason Adam and Robert Suarez, entrusted with high-leverage moments, each surrendered crushing home runs that derailed would-be wins. The late May and June stretch was particularly uneven — for every timely Bogaerts double or Jose Iglesias multi-RBI single, there was a Suarez blown save or an Adam meltdown that sent a close game sideways.\n\nThe second half steadied the ship. The Padres leaned on their pitching depth, received a signature Fernando Tatis Jr. grand slam in late September that crystallized the season's late-season resolve, and held off challengers to claim a Wild Card spot. Bradgley Rodriguez got crucial outs, the defense executed key double plays, and the team crossed the finish line at 90-72 with momentum they hoped to carry into October.\n\nThe Wild Card round against the Cubs, however, proved to be the season's cruel final chapter. Nick Pivetta surrendered back-to-back home runs in Game 1 that shifted the series' tone, and though Manny Machado delivered a two-run shot to keep hopes alive in Game 2, Yu Darvish couldn't hold a slim lead in the decisive game. A Freddy Fermin fly out ended San Diego's October with the quiet finality that Wild Card exits always carry — a good season, a hard stop, and a long winter to wonder what might have been.",
